---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-for-vegans`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D for Vegans',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D for Vegans', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D for Vegans: Lichen D3, D2, Fortified Foods & UV Mushrooms"
  description="Vegan-friendly vitamin D sources: lichen-derived cholecalciferol, fortified plant milks, UV-exposed mushrooms, and D2 vs D3 tradeoffs. Practical supplementation guide."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-for-vegans", label: "Vegan Vitamin D" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D for Vegans</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      A well-planned vegan diet covers most nutrients — but vitamin D is a genuine gap.
      Almost all natural D<sub>3</sub> (cholecalciferol) sources are animal, and D<sub>2</sub>
      (ergocalciferol) from fungi is less potent per IU. Vegans need to be deliberate.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Sources compared</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm mb-8">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-gray-50 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Source</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Form</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Typical dose</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-gray-700">
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Lichen-derived D<sub>3</sub></td><td class="px-4 py-3">Cholecalciferol</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,000–5,000 IU</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">The gold-standard vegan option — biochemically identical to lanolin-derived D<sub>3</sub>. Look for "vegan D3" on the label.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Ergocalciferol (D<sub>2</sub>)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Ergocalciferol</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,000–5,000 IU</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Yeast/fungal origin. Vegan by default. Raises serum 25(OH)D 60–90% less effectively per IU than D<sub>3</sub>.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">UV-exposed mushrooms</td><td class="px-4 py-3">D<sub>2</sub></td><td class="px-4 py-3">200–500 IU per serving</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">White, portabella, and shiitake mushrooms exposed to UV light contain meaningful D<sub>2</sub>. Look for "UV-exposed" or "vitamin D–enhanced" labelling.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Fortified soy/oat/almond milk</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Usually D<sub>2</sub>, sometimes D<sub>3</sub></td><td class="px-4 py-3">80–120 IU per cup</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Read the label — some brands use lichen D<sub>3</sub>, others D<sub>2</sub>.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Fortified orange juice</td><td class="px-4 py-3">D<sub>3</sub> or D<sub>2</sub></td><td class="px-4 py-3">100 IU per cup</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Common fortification; check the source.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Fortified breakfast cereals</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Usually D<sub>3</sub></td><td class="px-4 py-3">40–100 IU per serving</td><td class="px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Common D<sub>3</sub> in cereals comes from lanolin — check for vegan certification.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Should vegans take D<sub>2</sub> or lichen D<sub>3</sub>?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Lichen D<sub>3</sub> if you can afford it — it raises serum 25(OH)D more effectively
      per IU and produces steadier levels. D<sub>2</sub> works, but you need a higher dose
      (~1.5–2× the equivalent D<sub>3</sub> dose) and it clears faster, so trough levels
      dip lower between doses. Costs of lichen D<sub>3</sub> have fallen; a year's supply
      is now $20–40 in the US and UK.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sun exposure</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cutaneous synthesis works the same regardless of diet. Sensible sun exposure remains
      the most bioavailable vitamin D source for anyone during the UVB season. Vegans at
      high latitudes still hit the vitamin D winter (~November through February) and need
      supplementation during those months.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Testing considerations</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Standard 25(OH)D lab assays measure total 25(OH)D<sub>2</sub> + 25(OH)D<sub>3</sub>.
      If you supplement with D<sub>2</sub>, some older assays may underestimate your total
      status — modern LC-MS/MS methods measure both forms accurately. If you're on D<sub>2</sub>
      and your result is unexpectedly low, ask your lab about the assay used.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recommended vegan protocol</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Daily lichen-derived vitamin D<sub>3</sub> 1,000–2,000 IU with a fat-containing meal.</li>
      <li>Include UV-exposed mushrooms or fortified plant milks as regular dietary sources.</li>
      <li>Test serum 25(OH)D at end of winter (February–March) — vegans have higher deficiency rates in cohort studies.</li>
      <li>During UVB season, sensible sun exposure (10–30 minutes midday, 2–3× weekly).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Vegan diet planning benefits from consultation with
      a registered dietitian, especially for children, pregnant women, and people with
      malabsorption disorders.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-for-vegans" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
